ney": 0.0}, "rating_margin": 15.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.47, "baseline_margin": 16.1, "analyst_adjustment": -1.1, "key_factors": ["Consistent double-digit polling leads for Lamont, including a 20-point Quinnipiac poll in mid-October", "Strong Democratic state lean plus incumbency advantage", "All seven expert ratings place the race at Solid or Safe D", "Prediction markets price Lamont's win probability at 95-97%"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Lamont ahead by double digits, capped by Quinnipiac's 58-38 result, and markets and raters uniformly treat the race as decided. The fundamentals point to a mid-teens Democratic margin, so the forecast sits at roughly +15 with modest uncertainty.", "watch": "Fazio remains unknown to about three in ten voters, so a late debate moment or external spending surge could tighten the race, though not enough to threaten the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Ned Lamont seeks a third term against state Senator Ryan Fazio in a state that leans Democratic by about eight points. Polling has been one-sided all cycle: Quinnipiac's October survey shows Lamont up 58-38, and earlier polls from UNH and Quinnipiac showed leads of 17 to 20 points. Even the Republican-sponsored co/efficient poll had Lamont ahead by seven.\n\nRaters unanimously call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets put Lamont's chances at 95 percent or higher. Both campaigns have spent about $7 million on advertising, but Fazio's name recognition remains limited, with three in ten likely voters unfamiliar with him. The fundamentals — state lean, incumbency, and a favorable polling environment — all point the same direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin": 14.5}, {"